 2.1 First suppose $r/r_\varepsilon^*\to\infty$ and $r\to0$. Put $N=\lceil2/r\rceil$, $t_N=4\sqrt{\log N}$, and reject if $\max_{i\le N}|Y_i|/\varepsilon>t_N$. Under the null, [F5] and a union bound give type-I error at most $2N e^{-t_N^2/2}=2N^{-7}\to0$. For any alternative, $|\eta_i|\le i^{-2}$ follows from [F1], so the omitted weighted energy obeys $\sum_{i>N}i^2\eta_i^2\le N^{-2}\sum_{i>N}i^2|\eta_i|\le N^{-2}\le r^2/4$. Hence $\sum_{i\le N}i^2\eta_i^2\ge3r^2/4$. On the event that all $N$ null-noise coordinates satisfy $|\xi_i|\le t_N/2$, nonrejection would imply $|\eta_i|\le(3/2)\varepsilon t_N$ for every $i\le N$, and therefore $\sum_{i\le N}i^2\eta_i^2\le(3/2)\varepsilon t_N\sum_{i\le N}i^2|\eta_i|\le(3/2)\varepsilon t_N$. But $r/r_\varepsilon^*\to\infty$ makes $r^2/(\varepsilon\sqrt{\log N})\to\infty$, a contradiction for small $\varepsilon$. The probability that the stated noise event fails is at most $2N e^{-(t_N/2)^2/2}=2/N\to0$, so the maximal type-II error tends to zero. [F1, F3, F4, F5, step 1.1, algebra]
 
-2.2 For an arbitrary positive radius sequence with $r_\varepsilon/r_\varepsilon^*\to\infty$, use the positive representative $r_\varepsilon^*=\varepsilon^{1/2}(\log\varepsilon^{-1})^{1/4}$ and put $\bar r_\varepsilon=\min\{r_\varepsilon,\sqrt{r_\varepsilon^*}\}$. Then $\bar r_\varepsilon\to0$, $\bar r_\varepsilon\le r_\varepsilon$, and $\bar r_\varepsilon/r_\varepsilon^*=\min\{r_\varepsilon/r_\varepsilon^*,1/\sqrt{r_\varepsilon^*}\}\to\infty$. Apply step 2.1 with $\bar r_\varepsilon$ in place of $r_\varepsilon$. Its cutoff $N=\lceil2/\bar r_\varepsilon\rceil$ now tends to infinity, so both its null-size bound $2N^{-7}$ and its noise-event bound $2/N$ vanish, and the energy-tail and signal-to-threshold estimates in that step apply at $\bar r_\varepsilon$. Since $\Theta_1(r_\varepsilon)\subseteq\Theta_1(\bar r_\varepsilon)$, the same test has unchanged null size and no larger maximal type-II error on the requested alternative. Its total error therefore tends to zero, and its null size is eventually below any fixed significance level, giving both consistency limits in [F1]. For an empty requested alternative both minimax risks are zero by that definition, with the never-reject test attaining zero total error. This covers the nonvanishing-radius case without using a bounded or nondiverging cutoff. [F1, step 2.1, given, construct, algebra]
